============================================================
  PROPOSITION E: STAIRCASE ↔ ISO CLASS BIJECTION
============================================================

  Each tournament is a binary filling of the staircase δ_{n-1}.
  Two fillings are in the same iso class iff they differ by
  a permutation of rows and columns (= vertex relabeling).

  The iso class = the S_n-ORBIT of the staircase filling.

  The RSK correspondence maps each filling to:
    (P-tableau, Q-tableau) where P encodes structure, Q encodes labeling.
    Two fillings in the same iso class have the SAME P-tableau shape.

  So: iso class ↔ P-tableau shape (up to the RSK mapping).

  But the RSK is defined for PERMUTATION MATRICES, not binary matrices.
  The extension to binary matrices uses the Robinson-Schensted for
  {0,1}-matrices (the "RSK for 01-matrices" by Knuth).

  For a tournament (upper triangular 01-matrix):
    RSK gives (P, Q) where shapes are partitions of C(n,2).
    The shape λ = a PARTITION of C(n,2) = n(n-1)/2.
    Two tournaments in the same iso class share the P-shape? NO —
    RSK doesn't respect the tournament symmetry in this way.

  HOWEVER: the SCORE SEQUENCE is the ROW-SUM vector of the staircase.
  And score sequences correspond to COMPOSITIONS (ordered partitions).
  The sorted score = a PARTITION.

  So: score class ↔ partition of C(n,2) into n parts.
  And within each score class: the iso classes correspond to
  REFINEMENTS of the partition structure.

  SCORE → PARTITION AT n=5:

    Class 0 (H=1): scores = (0, 1, 2, 3, 4), sum = 10, partition of 10
    Class 1 (H=3): scores = (0, 1, 3, 3, 3), sum = 10, partition of 10
    Class 2 (H=3): scores = (0, 2, 2, 2, 4), sum = 10, partition of 10
    Class 3 (H=3): scores = (1, 1, 1, 3, 4), sum = 10, partition of 10
    Class 4 (H=5): scores = (0, 2, 2, 3, 3), sum = 10, partition of 10
    Class 5 (H=5): scores = (1, 1, 2, 2, 4), sum = 10, partition of 10
    Class 6 (H=9): scores = (1, 1, 2, 3, 3), sum = 10, partition of 10
    Class 7 (H=9): scores = (1, 1, 2, 3, 3), sum = 10, partition of 10
    Class 8 (H=11): scores = (1, 2, 2, 2, 3), sum = 10, partition of 10
    Class 9 (H=13): scores = (1, 2, 2, 2, 3), sum = 10, partition of 10
    Class 10 (H=15): scores = (1, 2, 2, 2, 3), sum = 10, partition of 10
    Class 11 (H=15): scores = (2, 2, 2, 2, 2), sum = 10, partition of 10

  COMPLEMENT DUALITY:
  If scores = (s₀,...,s₄), complement has scores = (4-s₄,...,4-s₀)

    Class 0 scores (0, 1, 2, 3, 4) ↔ complement (0, 1, 2, 3, 4) (class 0) [SC]
    Class 1 scores (0, 1, 3, 3, 3) ↔ complement (1, 1, 1, 3, 4) (class 3) [NSC]
    Class 2 scores (0, 2, 2, 2, 4) ↔ complement (0, 2, 2, 2, 4) (class 2) [SC]
    Class 3 scores (1, 1, 1, 3, 4) ↔ complement (0, 1, 3, 3, 3) (class 1) [NSC]
    Class 4 scores (0, 2, 2, 3, 3) ↔ complement (1, 1, 2, 2, 4) (class 5) [NSC]
    Class 5 scores (1, 1, 2, 2, 4) ↔ complement (0, 2, 2, 3, 3) (class 4) [NSC]
    Class 6 scores (1, 1, 2, 3, 3) ↔ complement (1, 1, 2, 3, 3) (class 6) [SC]
    Class 7 scores (1, 1, 2, 3, 3) ↔ complement (1, 1, 2, 3, 3) (class 6) [SC]
    Class 8 scores (1, 2, 2, 2, 3) ↔ complement (1, 2, 2, 2, 3) (class 8) [SC]
    Class 9 scores (1, 2, 2, 2, 3) ↔ complement (1, 2, 2, 2, 3) (class 8) [SC]
